    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T06:58:35Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Sport & Entertainment in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s competitive sports, living athletic traditions, community exercise, women’s participation, and connections with digital entertainment."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "sporting-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Several sporting worlds",
        "content": "Iranian sport becomes easier to understand when several different activities are kept in view. International championships record the achievements of selected athletes. Club competitions create recurring encounters between teams and supporters. Community exercise concerns opportunities to move in everyday life. Traditional practices can combine athletic training with forms of cultural transmission that a results table cannot capture. Iran’s Olympic record, UNESCO’s heritage descriptions, and the World Health Organization’s physical-activity framework illuminate different parts of this landscape.[s1][s2][s3][s4]\n\nThese distinctions matter because an internationally successful team does not establish how many people play its sport, and a medal total does not measure access to facilities. Equally, a practice preserved through families, local teachers, and community gatherings need not resemble a professional league to have sporting significance. This guide therefore treats achievement, participation, heritage, and entertainment as related subjects with different kinds of evidence.[s1][s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "international-competition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "International competition and its limits",
        "content": "Wrestling, weightlifting, and taekwondo occupy a prominent place in Iran’s Olympic history. Olympedia’s historical account identifies strength and combat sports as the main sources of the country’s Olympic success, while also recording achievements beyond them, including athletics, shooting, and karate. That breadth is useful: a familiar national sporting image should not make less visible disciplines disappear.[s1]\n\nTeam sports supply another perspective. Iran’s men won a third consecutive Asian Games volleyball title at Hangzhou in 2023, following victories at the previous editions in 2014 and 2018. The event retained the Hangzhou 2022 name despite being held the following year. Championship names and actual competition dates should therefore be read separately.[s5]\n\nResults establish what happened in a defined competition. They do not, by themselves, demonstrate the quality of every domestic club, the geographical distribution of coaching, or equal opportunities for aspiring athletes. The popular-sports branch examines individual disciplines, while the clubs-and-competitions branch provides a separate place to understand leagues and recurring tournaments.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "living-traditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Athletic traditions as living culture",
        "content": "Pahlevani and Zoorkhanei rituals illustrate why sporting heritage needs more than a list of champions. UNESCO describes a practice in which coordinated exercises, equipment associated symbolically with weapons, poetry, and drumming form a shared session. The training space is the zurkhaneh, commonly translated as a house of strength; UNESCO’s account describes a sunken arena within a domed building. A morshed leads the performance through recitation and rhythm.[s2]\n\nThe same account emphasizes ethical instruction and assistance to people in need. These are elements of the tradition’s documented self-understanding, rather than evidence that every training establishment operates identically. UNESCO inscribed the rituals on its Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2010. Inscription recognizes a living cultural practice; it does not turn a historical nomination’s facility count into a current national census.[s2]\n\nChogān offers a different combination of skills. Riders use wooden sticks to move a ball toward the opposing team’s goal, while music and storytelling belong to the wider cultural practice. UNESCO identifies players, storytellers, and musicians as its principal bearer groups. Its account describes transmission within families and workshops, alongside associations that organize teaching and support practitioners. Inscribed in 2017, chogān is consequently both an equestrian game and a tradition carried through performance and instruction.[s3]\n\nThese examples also caution against presenting Iranian sporting heritage as a single unchanged inheritance. Different practices have different settings, participants, and methods of transmission. The traditional-sports branch separates those subjects so that their particular histories remain visible.[s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "participation-and-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation, spectatorship, and access",
        "content": "The World Health Organization defines physical activity broadly enough to include movement during transport, work, domestic activities, recreation, and sport. Walking or cycling therefore belongs in an account of active life even when it produces no membership record or competitive result. This distinction gives the community-sport branch a different purpose from coverage of elite performance.[s4]\n\nWatching sport raises a separate access question. Associated Press reporting on the Tehran football derby in December 2023 documented women attending the match after decades of extensive exclusion from men’s sporting events. It also reported activists’ concerns about restricted ticket availability and harassment. Admission at a particular fixture should consequently be described as a dated development, rather than proof of unrestricted access across all venues.[s6]\n\nWomen’s sport also requires coverage of athletes, coaching, leagues, and facilities in their own right. Spectator access is one part of that subject, not an adequate substitute for it.",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "entertainment-and-navigation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where entertainment overlaps with competition",
        "content": "Digital entertainment introduces another distinction: playing games recreationally, producing games commercially, and competing in esports are different activities. The Olympic Council of Asia records esports becoming an official medal sport at the Hangzhou Asian Games in 2023. That establishes its place in a regional competition programme; it supplies no estimate of Iran’s gaming audience or domestic industry revenue.[s7]\n\nThe directory’s branches offer complementary routes into the subject. Popular sports explains disciplines; traditional sports examines inherited practices; clubs and competitions follows organized contests. Community sport and women’s sport address participation and opportunity. Sports industry and digital entertainment concern commercial and media activity, for which separate Iranian evidence is still needed. Reading across these branches avoids reducing the sporting landscape to either medals or famous clubs.",
        "source_ids": ["s7"]
      }
    ],
