    "description": "Football, volleyball, basketball, wrestling, weightlifting, and taekwondo provide different entry points into Iranian competitive sport. Their histories combine continental team success, Olympic achievement, and distinctive sporting traditions, without supplying a single reliable ranking of nationwide participation.",
    "tags": ["Iranian athletes", "Football", "Volleyball", "Basketball", "Wrestling", "Olympic sports"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T06:58:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T06:58:35Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Popular Sports in Iran: Teams and Olympic Disciplines",
      "description": "Understand Iranian football, volleyball, basketball, wrestling, weightlifting, and taekwondo through documented sporting achievements and distinctions."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "understanding-prominence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What makes a sport prominent?",
        "content": "A sport can be prominent through spectatorship, international results, participation, or a long-established cultural position. These measures answer different questions. Iran’s Olympic history emphasizes wrestling, weightlifting, and taekwondo, while its men’s volleyball and basketball teams have built substantial continental records. Football supplies another history of international competition and domestic club allegiance.[s1][s2][s3][s6]\n\nThe six disciplines grouped here should therefore be understood as subjects to explore, rather than places in a statistical popularity table. The inspected sources document sporting achievements much more securely than they establish comparable national participation rates. A championship title is strong evidence of success at that event; it cannot establish which activity is most widely played.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "team-sport-pathways",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different routes to team success",
        "content": "Football’s continental breakthrough preceded those of the other team sports considered here. Iran won the men’s Asian Cup in 1968 and retained the title in 1972. The Asian Football Confederation’s historical account describes the second championship as a successful defence in Bangkok, following the earlier victory on home soil. These tournaments provide a starting point for understanding the national team’s place in Asian football.[s6]\n\nMen’s volleyball developed a particularly strong continental record during the following decades. In September 2021, Iran defeated Japan in straight sets to retain the Asian championship and claim its fourth title. The final also illustrates a useful distinction between match statistics and outcomes: Japan recorded more successful attacks, while Iran’s advantages in blocking, serving, and limiting errors helped produce the victory. No single performance statistic explains an entire contest.[s2]\n\nBasketball’s landmark generation was built around players including Hamed Haddadi, a center whose role combined scoring near the basket, rebounding, and creating opportunities for teammates. FIBA’s retrospective identifies Iran’s first Asian championship in 2007, another title in 2009, and a third in 2013. Iran subsequently finished runner-up in 2017. Haddadi’s influence was thus sustained across several tournaments, rather than confined to a single exceptional result.[s3]\n\nThese records show different competitive trajectories. They should not be collapsed into a general claim that all Iranian national teams have the same international standing, or that continental success automatically produces equivalent results at a global championship.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "combat-and-strength",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Wrestling, weightlifting, and taekwondo",
        "content": "Olympedia identifies strength and combat disciplines as the main sources of Iran’s Olympic success. Weightlifter Hossein Reza Zadeh won Olympic gold in 2000 and 2004, while taekwondo athlete Hadi Saei is another Iranian double Olympic champion. These examples help explain why the country’s sporting identity cannot be understood from football alone.[s1]\n\nWrestling also demands attention to its internal distinctions. Freestyle and Greco-Roman are separate competitive styles, and a result in one is not interchangeable with a result in the other. United World Wrestling’s account of the Paris 2024 Greco-Roman final describes Mohammad Hadi Saravi defeating Artur Aleksanyan in the 97-kilogram division. Saravi’s comments connected his achievement with the wrestling community of his home city and province, illustrating the local affiliations that can sit behind a national result.[s4]\n\nHistorical accounts of the zurkhaneh distinguish its exercises and associated wrestling traditions from modern competition categories. Encyclopaedia Iranica describes the adoption of international age categories and weight classes alongside efforts to organize traditional exercises into scored competitions. Recognizing those changes is more informative than assuming that contemporary Olympic wrestling reproduces an unchanged ancient practice.[s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-records",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to read the sporting record",
        "content": "Begin with the discipline, the competition, and the team or athlete concerned. A men’s continental championship does not establish the record of the women’s team; an Olympic medal describes a particular event and weight division. The sources used here are strongest when read at that level of specificity.[s1][s2][s3][s4]\n\nThe individual sport pages provide space for those distinctions. Football requires attention to clubs as well as national teams. Volleyball and basketball have their own tournament histories. Wrestling, weightlifting, and taekwondo require careful reading of styles, divisions, and event results. Together, these approaches explain competitive achievement without turning it into an unsupported claim about the sporting habits of the whole population.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
